# Unlocking Efficient High‐Order Multiphoton‐Excited Fluorescence of Metal‐Organic Framework via Octupolar Module In Situ Formation

## abstract
Engineering high‐order multiphoton excited fluorescent (H‐MPEF) materials is of significant importance for sensing, 3D optical data storage and bioimaging. However, it remains a challenging endeavor due to a lack of appropriate construction strategies. This study demonstrates that incorporating octupolar modules within metal‐organic framework (MOF) offers new possibilities for the design of highly attractive H‐MPEF materials. Constructed from a typical MOF (UiO(bpdc)), the in situ formation of multibranched octupolar cyclometallated iridium(III) modules via post‐ligand coordination modification endowed the framework with enlarged static hyperpolarizabilities, an extended conjugated system, and enhanced charge transfer, ultimately unlocking its second near‐infrared (NIR‐II, 1000–1700 nm) light activated H‐MPEF performance including three‐ and four‐photon activity. Moreover, these exciting features, combined with subsequent orotic acid‐capping, enabled its application in cancer cell‐specific targeting oncotherapy using tissue‐penetrating NIR‐II light. This finding highlights the vital role of octupoles in H‐MPEF performance and sets a benchmark for unlocking the multiphoton activity of MOFs at the molecular level for deep‐seated tumor fluorescence imaging and therapy.

Engineering high-order multiphoton excited fluorescent (H-MPEF) materials is of significant importance for sensing, 3D optical data storage and bioimaging. However, it remains a challenging endeavor due to a lack of appropriate construction strategies. This study demonstrates that incorporating octupolar modules within metal-organic framework (MOF) offers new possibilities for the design of highly attractive H-MPEF materials. Constructed from a typical MOF (UiO(bpdc)), the in situ formation of multibranched octupolar cyclometallated iridium(III) modules via post-ligand coordination modification endowed the framework with enlarged static hyperpolarizabilities, an extended conjugated system, and enhanced charge transfer, ultimately unlocking its second near-infrared (NIR-II, 1000–1700 nm) light activated H-MPEF performance including three- and four-photon activity. Moreover, these exciting features, combined with subsequent orotic acid-capping, enabled its application in cancer cell-specific targeting oncotherapy using tissue-penetrating NIR-II light. This finding highlights the vital role of octupoles in H-MPEF performance and sets a benchmark for unlocking the multiphoton activity of MOFs at the molecular level for deep-seated tumor fluorescence imaging and therapy.
## 1. Introduction
High-order multi-photon $(n~\geq~3)$ excited fluorescent (H-MPEF) materials offer significant advantages over traditional singlephoton and reported two-photon fluorescent materials. They can absorb three or more much lower-energy photons simultaneously, resulting in high-energy upconversion emission with stronger spatial confinement, less Rayleigh scattering, smaller biological damage, and deeper penetration depth.[1] In this regards, they hold particularly advantages in sensing, 3D optical data storage, and bioimaging applications, while the development has been hindered by a lack of appropriate construction strategies.[2] In general, the related works were focused on developing large $\pi$ -conjugated systems involving complex synthesis and purification.[3] More importantly, the deficiency of multiphoton crosssection $(\delta_{\mathrm{n}}),^{[4]}$ fluorescent brightness as well as photostability hindered their further applications. As a whole, the access to H-MPEF materials with desired performance is challenging yet and needs complicated synthesis procedures. Therefore, open up facile ways to fabricate efficient H-MPEF materials is a highly desired target.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have shown great potential in the fabrication of multiphoton absorption (MPA) materials due to their prior features from metal nodes or/and organic linkers as well as their flexible functional sites and rational designable structures.[5] Qian’s group reported a two-photon responsive MOF for fluorescence patterning and imaging in high-resolution 3D structure by introducing nonlinear optical linkers.[6] Fischer and coworkers as well as Pan’s group developed serial MPA responsive MOFs using two-photon-active tetraphenylethene-based chromophore linkers.[2b,7] Very recently, we reported the first H-MPEF responsive MOF for bioimaging by taken multiphoton active thiazolothiazol-based unit as organic linker.[8] These works demonstrated that the MPA including H-MPEF activity of MOFs could be controlled at the molecular level via incorporation of photoactive linkers straightforwardly. In this sense, we envisage that the modification of organic linkers could be specifically designed for MPA, particularly HMPEF-responsive MOFs, to offer a unique access to a “targeteddesign” of this property at the molecular level. Unfortunately, valid post-ligand modification strategies for H-MPEF applications are still absent.
As powerful platforms for post-ligand coordination modification, MOFs with bipyridyl sites have been widely used in catalysis and biological fields yet for MPA applications are very rarely.[9] In fact, known as octupoles, the formed heteroleptic complexes with octahedral/tetrahedral geometry are attractive to MPA materials research due to their improved nonlinear optical coefficient compared with the conventional dipolar molecules.[4,10] However, the role of octupoles in H-MPEF performance has yet to be elaborated. Herein, the typical MOF containing bipyridyl sites, UiO(bpdc) (formulated $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{bpdc})_{6}$ , bpdc $=2{,}2^{\prime}$ - bipyridine- $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid), was selected as a model to investigate the crucial role of post-ligand coordination modification in H-MPEF performance (Scheme 1). First, inspired by the intriguing optical and pharmacological properties of benzothiazole derivatives,[11] a benzothiazole-based cyclometallated iridium(III) complex (termed as $[\mathbf{Ir}(\mathbf{PBTF})_{2}\mathbf{Cll}_{2})$ (Scheme S1, Figures S1 and S2, Supporting Information) was employed in post synthetic metalation endowing UiO(bpdc) with cationic moiety and strong spin-orbit coupling for mitochondria-targeting effective photodynamic therapy (PDT).[12] Of particular importance here are the multi-branched octupolar modules derived from $[\mathbf{Ir}(\mathbf{PBTF})_{2}\mathbf{Cl}]_{2}$ which could be in situ formation within UiO(bpdc) (termed as MIr) to optimize its nonlinear optical coefficient for desired H-MPEF performance (Scheme S2, Supporting Information). To our knowledge, such design idea has never been demonstrated in previous MOF-related studies.
## 2. Results and Discussion
As revealed by density functional theory (DFT) calculations via the sum-over-states (SOS) method,[13] the incorporation of such multibranched octupolar modules within MOFs afforded MIr with significantly improved nonlinear optical coefficient (Figure 1a). As shown in Figure 1b,c, the calculated static first hyperpolarizabilities $(\beta)$ and static second hyperpolarizabilities (𝛾) values of MIr were much larger than those of $[\mathbf{Ir}(\mathbf{PBTF})_{2}\mathbf{Cl}]_{2}$ and UiO(bpdc). Furthermore, the electron distribution (Figure 1d) suggested that the charge transfer existed within MIr. The lower band gap of MIr calculated by CASTEP (Figure S3, Supporting
Information) implied its enhanced charge transfer ability, which would improve the nonlinear optical performance.[14] In general, thanks to the octupolar module embedding, the improved nonlinear optical coefficient and charge transfer ability make MIr a promising MPA-responsive material.
Encouraged by the promising calculation results, we embarked on the preparation of the target materials. Initially, nanoscale UiO(bpdc) with sub- $.100\mathrm{nm}$ was obtained for the priority of H-MPEF materials in biological application. Next, the nanoscale UiO(bpdc) was treated with $[\mathbf{Ir}(\mathbf{PBTF})_{2}\mathbf{Cl}]_{2}$ yield MIr by a post-ligand coordination modification method. Last, the obtained MIr was coated with orotic acid (OA) by coordination effect between Zr and O atoms to offer MIrA with active-targeting ability for effective therapy (Figure 2a). The powder XRD patterns, SEM and TEM images of as-synthesized UiO(bpdc), MIr, and MIrA were displayed in Figure 2c and Figure S4, Supporting Information. The no noticeable change of PXRD patterns and morphology demonstrated that the structure of UiO(bpdc) maintained well after Ir-metalation and OA modification. More importantly, the element mapping analysis of UiO(bpdc) and MIr (Figure 2b, Figure S5, Supporting Information) illustrated the successful incorporation of Ir-module via high-yielding reaction (loading content was calculated to be $54\%$ via ICP-MS), which was further confirmed by Brunner–Emmet–Teller measurements, $\mathrm{^1H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy and inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (Figures S6–S8, Table S1, Supporting Information, the detailed discussions have been provided in the supporting information file). The functionalization efficiency of orotic acid was calculated to be $22.3\%$ according to the element analysis (Table S2, Supporting Information). In addition, dynamic light scattering (DLS) measurements of MIrA gave the zeta potential of $-36.88\ \mathrm{mV}$ in deionized water (Figure 2d, Figure S9, Supporting Information) and the particle size did not show any significant difference in Dulbecco’s Modified Eagle’s Medium (DMEM, cell culture medium) and phosphate buffer solution (PBS) for 7 days, confirming the good stability and dispersity of MIrA (Figure S10, Supporting Information) in physiological condition.
Subsequently, the optical spectrum of the obtained samples was collected. Notably, the OA modification did not affect the optical nature of MIr (the corresponding data was supplemented in Figures S11 and S12, Supporting Information), herein, we mainly discussed the optical performance of MIrA. Obviously, the absorption peak of MIrA displayed a red-shift of $30\ \mathrm{nm}$ given the extended conjugated system upon the incorporation of octupolar Ir-module (Figure 3a). Of note, MIrA showed bright fluorescence emission, with a fluorescence quantum yield $(\eta)$ of $31.57\%$ , which was much higher than that of $[\mathrm{Ir}(\mathrm{PBTF})_{2}\mathrm{Cll}]_{2}$ $(1.19\%)$ (Figure 3b). The extended conjugated system of MIrA is crucial for its MPA performance and its bright fluorescence emission is pivotal in multiphoton-excited fluorescence (MPEF) activity. In view of the improved nonlinear optical coefficient, enhanced charge transfer and extended conjugated system of MIrA, it is envisioned that the incorporation of such octupolar modules in UiO(bpdc) without MPA activity would unlock its MPA performance. As expected, MIrA exhibited obvious reverse saturable absorption response (Figure 3c), and its $\operatorname{Im}\chi^{(3)}$ value was $5.49\times10^{-14}$ esu, which was higher than that of $[\mathrm{Ir}(\mathrm{PBTF})_{2}\mathrm{Cll}]_{2}$ $(2.42\times10^{-14}$ esu). Then, MPEF activity of MIrA was evaluated. It’s worth noting that MIrA showed a typical MPEF performance under NIR laser excitation $(700~-~1600~\mathrm{nm})$ (Figure 3d–f). The slopes of $\mathrm{logI_{\mathrm{in}}}$ versus $\mathrm{logI_{out}}$ for MIrA were calculated as 1.72, 3.04 and 3.93 under 850, 1250, and $1450~\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, demonstrating its two-, three- and four-photon excited fluorescence behavior (Figure S13, Supporting Information). In contrast, $[\mathbf{Ir}(\mathbf{PB}\mathbf{TF})_{2}\mathbf{Cll}]_{2}$ exhibited ignorable MPEF signal due to the intense intramolecular electron transfer. These results demonstrated that the incorporation of octupolar modules within MOF strengthened its MPA performance and unlocked its MPEF, especially H-MPEF behavior (Figure S14, Supporting Information). Moreover, the multiphoton absorption cross-section of MIrA was evaluated by MPEF method,[15] and the $\delta_{\mathrm{n}}$ values were calculated to be $\delta_{2\mathrm{PA}}=7921.15\mathrm{GM}$ $(\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=850\mathrm{nm})$ , $\delta_{\mathrm{3PA}}=2.6\times$ $10^{-80}~\mathrm{cm}^{6}~\mathrm{s}^{2}$ photon−2 $(\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=1250\ \mathrm{nm})$ , and $\delta_{\mathrm{4PA}}=2.67\ \times$ $10^{-79}{\mathrm{~cm}}^{8}{\mathrm{~s}}^{3}$ photon−3 $(\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=1450\ \mathrm{nm})$ ), respectively. The much larger $\eta\delta_{\mathrm{2PA}}$ value (the product of $\eta$ and two-photon absorption cross-section, the parameter to evaluate the two-photon excited fluorescent brightness, 2500.07 GM) than the widely used criterion (50 GM), much larger high-order MPA cross-sections than those of reported and widely used materials, as well as excellent photostability of MIrA (Figure S15, Supporting Information), make it a prior candidate for MPEF imaging, especially NIR-II light activated H-MPEF imaging.
Indeed, fluorescence confocal microscopy imaging indicated that MIrA could be employed as an effective tool for NIR-II light activated H-MPEF imaging (excitation wavelength: $1250\mathrm{nm}$ ) and selectively targeted OAT2 receptors-overexpressing cancer cells (human hepatoma cells: Hep G2 cells),[16] rather than OAT2 receptors-negative cells (human embryonic kidney cells: Hek $293\mathrm{T}$ cells), due to its considerable MPEF performance and the surface modification by orotic acid (Figures S16 and S17, Table S3, Supporting Information further showed the related result of ICP-MS assay). Moreover, thanks to the cyclometallated iridium(III) bridging ligands and cationic feature of MIr in tumor microenvironment (Table S4, Supporting Information),[17] MIrA displayed intrinsic mitochondria-targeting capability given the distribution of which in cells was consistent with that of mitotracker green (pearson’s colocalization coefficient value is 0.96, Figure 4a). In addition, obviously cell apoptosis could be observed upon light irradiation (Figure S18, Supporting Information) by MIrA, due to the excellent light-induced reactive oxygen species (ROS) generation ability (related spectroscopy and electron spin resonance assays were shown in Supporting information file Figures S19–S21, Supporting Information) resulting from the strong spin-orbit coupling and linker-to-cluster charge transfer. Then, the intracellular NIR-II light activated ROS generation ability and in vitro PDT outcome of MIrA were evaluated using H-MPEF imaging. As illustrated in Figure 4b, the distinctly emission signals of singlet oxygen sensor green (SOSG, ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ indicator) and dihydroethidium (DHE, ${\sf O}_{2}^{\bullet-}$ indicator) in MIrA-treated Hep G2 cells demonstrated the considerable intracellular ROS generation ability of MIrA. Moreover, it’s in vitro PDT outcome was studied using Hep G2 cells stained with calcein acetoxymethyl ester (calcein AM, green fluorescence, indicating live cells) and propidium iodide (PI, red fluorescence, indicating dead cells). Evidently red fluorescent signal of PI from dead cells could be collected with light irradiation time prolonged suggesting the impressive PDT efficacy of MIrA (Figure 4c and Figure S22, Supporting Information), which was further confirmed by standard methyl-thiazole diphenyl tetrazolium bromide and flow cytometry assays (Figure S23–S24, Supporting
Information). Benefiting from the mitochondria-targeting capability of MIrA, the pathway of cell apoptosis was investigated with commercial dye JC-10 as a mitochondrial membrane potential indicator by comparing the emission signals of J-monomer (green channel, predominantly distributed in cytosol) and J-aggregates (red channel, located in intact mitochondrial membrane) under H-MPEF imaging (Figure 4d). The sharply attenuated red fluorescence of MIrA-treated cells exposed to NIR-II light could be observed ( $1250~\mathrm{nm}$ , $0.1\mathrm{~W~cm}^{-2}$ ). In addition, the expression cleaved caspased-3 which is the hallmark of apoptosis located in mitochondrial of the group treated with both MIrA and NIR-II light was much higher than that of other groups (Figure S25, Supporting Information). The above results revealed that the lightinduced ROS generation caused depolarization of the mitochondrial membrane and then induced cell apoptosis (Figure S26, Supporting Information).
Remarkably, one-, two-, and three-photon fluorescence (1PF, 2PF, and 3PF) imaging of MIrA using cells and tissue elaborated that MPEF imaging especially NIR-II light activated 3PEF (H-MPEF) imaging exhibited much stronger fluorescence intensity, higher resolution and deeper penetration depth compared with 1PF and 2PF imaging (Figure 5a–c, Figure S27, Supporting Information). Combined with its cancer cell-specific targeting ability and excellent ROS generation behavior, MIrA offers ample possibilities for deeper cancer imaging and therapy. Furthermore, treatment of MIrA in deep tumors using Hep G2 3D multicellular spheroids (3D MCSs) was verified, where MCSs were treated with MIrA and stained with calcein AM/PI (Figure 5d–e). The one/two-photon-treated MCSs showed red fluorescence from PI only at the edge of the MCSs and only green fluorescence from calcein AM at the center of the tumor. In contrast, the red fluorescence of the MIrA under $1250\mathrm{nm}$ excitation (three-photon group) was distributed throughout the tumor section and was clearly visible even in the center of the tumor. These results indicate that NIR-II light-induced photodynamic therapy is effective in the treatment of deep tumors.
Inspired by the superior ability of in vitro fluorescence imaging and anticancer effect, the in vivo cancer targeting and PDT behavior of MIrA was further evaluated toward the H22 cancer bearing mice (Figure 6a). First, the hemolysis ratio of MIrA was calculated to be less than $0.9\%$ (intravenous injectable is generally considered safe when the hemolysis rate is less than $5\%$ ), which verified its excellent biocompatibility for further biological applications (Figure S28, Supporting Information). Then, the tumor targeting and accumulation ability of MIrA was evaluated. As shown in Figure S29, Supporting Information, obvious fluorescence from MIrA was observed after intravenous injection for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ and steadily increased until reaching its peak at $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The tumors and major organs of the mice bearing H22 tumors were harvested $24\mathrm{{h}}$ post injection to measure $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{zr}}$ concentration by ICP-MS assay. As shown in Figure S30, Supporting Information, a high concentration of $\mathrm{zr}$ was observed in tumor tissue treated with MIrA, exhibiting the effective intratumoral accumulation of MIrA. Moreover, the pharmacokinetics of MIrA were initially assessed by intravenous injection as well, exhibiting a relatively long lifetime (3.1 h) within the bloodstream (Figure 6b). Meanwhile, standard blood parameters were also analyzed, and no significant abnormalities in blood biochemical indexes were observed in the control and MIrA groups (Figure S31, Supporting Information). The above results indicated that MIrA with good biosafety accumulates effectively in tumor regions.
The efficiency of anticancer of MIrA under NIR-II light irradiation was assessed through H22 tumor-bearing mice. The tumorbearing mice were treated with PBS, NIR-II light, and MIrA with/without NIR-II light irradiation, respectively. The mice were irradiated with $1250~\mathrm{nm}$ laser after intravenous injection. After 2 weeks of treatments, it can be clearly observed from the tumor growth curve and tumor photos of the mice that the group of MIrA with NIR-II light irradiation achieved obvious tumor inhibition, which further proves that MIrA has a good tumor inhibition effect under NIR-II laser irradiation (Figure 6c and Figure S32 and S33, Supporting Information). Hematoxylin and eosin (H&E), terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase dUTP nick end labeling (TUNEL), Ki-67, and Hochest-33258 staining experiments were performed using tumor sections. The results showed that the MIrA treated with NIR-II light group had an effectively inhibitory effect on tumor proliferation compared with the other groups (Figure 6e and Figure S34, Supporting Information). Moreover, the body weights of the mice had no obvious changes during the 14 days of treatment (Figure 6d), and H&E staining of heart, liver, spleen, lung, kidney tissue revealed no noticeable abnormality or apparent organ damage (Figure S35, Supporting Information).[18] As a result, MIrA has been proven to be a powerful platform for NIR-II light activated H-MPEF imaging and PDT by virtue of its considerable H-MPEF performance and ROS generation ability. All the animal procedures were approved by the Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee of Anhui University (serial number: 2020-042) based on the National
Standard of China GB/T35892-2018 guidelines for Ethical Review of Experimental Animal Welfare.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we proposed a high-yielding post-ligand coordination modification strategy for unlocking NIR-II light activated H-MPEF activity through in situ incorporation of multibranch octupolar chromophore into the metal-organic framework. Thanks to the enlarged static hyperpolarizabilities, extended conjugated system, and enhanced charge transfer, the obtained MOF exhibited considerable high-order multiphoton activity, especially emission behavior under NIR-II light irradiation. Besides, the Ir-metalation and OA modification afforded its strong mitochondria-targeting property for effective cancer cell-specific targeting oncotherapy using NIR-II light. This is the first report elaborating the role of octupoles played in high-order MPA performance, which provides a simple and facile way to unlock high-order MPA performance at the molecular level and offers ample possibilities for cancer diagnosis and treatment using tissue-penetrating NIR-II light.
